{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 36, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "**Cherry Blossoms and Neon Lights: My Week in Japan**\n\nStepping off the plane in Tokyo felt like entering a different dimension. The air hummed with energy, a unique blend of ancient tradition and futuristic innovation. My first stop was Shibuya Crossing, where thousands of people moved in synchronized chaos under a sea of neon signs. It was overwhelming yet mesmerizing.\n\nThe next day, I traded the city noise for the serene beauty of Kyoto. Walking through the Fushimi Inari Shrine, surrounded by thousands of vibrant orange torii gates winding up the mountain, felt spiritual. The silence was broken only by the rustling of leaves and distant temple bells. Later, I witnessed the peak of sakura season in Maruyama Park. Pink petals drifted down like snow, covering the ground in a soft carpet while families enjoyed hanami picnics beneath the trees.\n\nFood was, of course, a highlight. From steaming bowls of rich tonkotsu ramen in tiny alleyway shops to delicate kaiseki dinners that looked too beautiful to eat, every meal was an adventure. I even tried my hand at a tea ceremony, learning the precise, meditative movements required to whisk matcha to perfection.\n\nWhat struck me most was the omotenashi, the Japanese concept of wholehearted hospitality. Strangers bowed politely, offered help without being asked, and treated every interaction with genuine care.\n\nLeaving Japan was bittersweet. I came home with a camera full of photos, a suitcase full of snacks, and a heart full of memories. It's a place where the past and future coexist beautifully, and I already know I'll be back to explore more of its hidden gems.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.04935571}, {"token": "Ch", "logprob": -0.132839}, {"token": "erry", "logprob": -0.25194123}, {"token": " Bloss", "logprob": -1.6689314e-06}, {"token": "oms", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob":